67,4.5561224489795915,4.637755102040816,4.719387755102041,4.801020408163265,4.88265306122449,4.9642857142857135,5.045918367346939,5.127551020408163,5.209183673469387,5.290816326530612,5.372448979591836,5.454081632653061,5.535714285714285,5.61734693877551,5.698979591836734,5.780612244897959,5.862244897959183,5.943877551020408,6.025510204081632,6.107142857142857,6.188775510204081,6.270408163265305,6.3520408163265305,6.433673469387754,6.51530612244898,6.596938775510203,6.678571428571428,6.7602040816326525,6.841836734693877,6.923469387755102,7.005102040816326,7.086734693877551],[3.249999999999999,3.3316326530612237,3.4132653061224483,3.494897959183673,3.576530612244897,3.6581632653061216,3.739795918367346,3.8214285714285703,3.903061224489795,3.9846938775510194,4.066326530612244,4.147959183673469,4.229591836734693,4.311224489795917,4.392857142857142,4.474489795918366,4.556122448979591,4.637755102040815,4.71938775510204,4.801020408163264,4.882653061224489,4.9642857142857135,5.045918367346937,5.127551020408163,5.209183673469386,5.290816326530612,5.3724489795918355,5.45408163265306,5.535714285714285,5.617346938775509,5.698979591836734,5.7806122448979576,5.862244897959183,5.943877551020407,6.025510204081632,6.107142857142856,6.1887755102040805,6.270408163265305,6.35204081632653,6.433673469387754,6.515306122448978,6.596938775510203,6.678571428571427,6.7602040816326525,6.841836734693876,6.923469387755101,7.005102040816325,7.08673469387755,7.1683673469387745,7.249999999999998]],\"type\":\"surface\",\"name\":\"1.0*(y-0.5)+2.0*(x-1.0)+1.25\"}],\"layout\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"Tangent plane at (1, 0.5)\"},\"width\":700,\"height\":450,\"paper_bgcolor\":\"white\",\"scene\":{\"bgcolor\":\"white\",\"xaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"x\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"yaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"y\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1},\"zaxis\":{\"title\":{\"text\":\"z\"},\"gridcolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linecolor\":\"#ddd\",\"linewidth\":1,\"zerolinecolor\":\"black\",\"zerolinewidth\":1}}}}"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Partials exist ≠ differentiable\n\nA subtlety with no 1D analogue: a function can have all partial derivatives at a point and *still* not be differentiable there.\n\nTake $f(x, y) = xy/(x^2 + y^2)$ for $(x, y) \\ne 0$, $f(0, 0) = 0$. Both partials at the origin are 0:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* f(x, 0) = 0 for all x, so ∂f/∂x at origin = lim (f(h, 0) - f(0,0))/h = 0\n   Similarly for ∂f/∂y. But f isn't even continuous at origin (previous notebook),\n   let alone differentiable. */\nprint(\"∂f/∂x at origin = lim_{h→0} (f(h, 0) - 0)/h = 0   (f vanishes on the x-axis)\")$\nprint(\"∂f/∂y at origin = 0   (similarly on y-axis)\")$\nprint(\"But f isn't continuous at origin, so it can't be differentiable.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 63,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∂f/∂x at origin = lim_{h→0} (f(h, 0) - 0)/h = 0   (f vanishes on the x-axis)\n∂f/∂y at origin = 0   (similarly on y-axis)\nBut f isn't continuous at origin, so it can't be differentiable."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "**Sufficient condition** for differentiability: if all partials exist *and are continuous* in a neighbourhood of $x_0$, then $f$ is differentiable at $x_0$ (in fact $C^1$). This is the criterion you actually use in practice.\n\n## Chain rule in matrix form\n\nFor composable functions $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$ and $g : \\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}^k$, both differentiable, the composition $g \\circ f$ is differentiable with\n\n$$D(g \\circ f)(x) = Dg(f(x)) \\cdot Df(x).$$\n\nThe 1D chain rule $(g \\circ f)' = g'(f) \\cdot f'$ is the special case $n = m = k = 1$. The matrix form generalises naturally.\n\nMaxima can verify this on a concrete example.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* f: ℝ² → ℝ²,  g: ℝ² → ℝ */\nf_inner : [x^2 + y, x*y]$\ng_outer : (u + v)^2$\nJ_f : genmatrix(lambda([i, j], diff(f_inner[i], state_vars[j])), 2, 2)$\nJ_g : matrix([diff(g_outer, u), diff(g_outer, v)])$\nprint(\"J_f =\")$ J_f;\nprint(\"J_g (in u, v) =\")$ J_g;\n\n/* Compose:  h(x, y) = g(f(x, y)) = (x²+y + xy)²  */\nh_composed : subst([u = f_inner[1], v = f_inner[2]], g_outer)$\nJ_h_direct : matrix([diff(h_composed, x), diff(h_composed, y)])$\nprint(\"∂(g∘f)/∂(x,y) directly =\")$\nexpand(J_h_direct);\n\n/* Chain rule: J_h = J_g(at f) · J_f */\nJ_g_at_f : subst([u = f_inner[1], v = f_inner[2]], J_g)$\nJ_h_via_chain : J_g_at_f . J_f$\nprint(\"∂(g∘f)/∂(x,y) via chain rule =\")$\nexpand(J_h_via_chain);\n\nprint(\"Matches:\", is(equal(expand(J_h_direct), expand(J_h_via_chain))))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 64,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "J_f =\nmatrix([2*x,1],[y,x])\nJ_g (in u, v) =\nmatrix([2*(v+u),2*(v+u)])\n∂(g∘f)/∂(x,y) directly =\nmatrix([2*x*y^2+2*y^2+6*x^2*y+4*x*y+4*x^3,2*x^2*y+4*x*y+2*y+2*x^3+2*x^2])\n∂(g∘f)/∂(x,y) via chain rule =\nmatrix([2*x*y^2+2*y^2+6*x^2*y+4*x*y+4*x^3,2*x^2*y+4*x*y+2*y+2*x^3+2*x^2])\nMatches: true"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Why the matrix view matters\n\nThe chain rule expressed as matrix multiplication generalises seamlessly to:\n\n- **Backpropagation** in neural networks (the chain rule applied through stacked layers).\n- **Coordinate changes** in physics — the metric tensor transforms by Jacobian *congruence*, $g' = J^{\\mathsf{T}} g\\, J$. (Congruence, not conjugation: $g$ is a bilinear form, not an operator.)\n- **Implicit function theorem** — if $\\partial F/\\partial y$ is invertible, you can solve $F(x, y) = 0$ locally for $y(x)$ (next notebook).\n\nThe total-derivative perspective also reveals what \"differentiable\" really means: not \"you can take any partial\", but \"you have a coherent linear approximation that works in *every* direction simultaneously.\" The 1D case hides this because there's only one direction.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Reference\n\nZorich, V.A. (2016) *Mathematical Analysis II*. 2nd edn. Berlin, Heidelberg: Springer Berlin Heidelberg. doi:[10.1007/978-3-662-48993-2](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-662-48993-2) — ch. 10, *Differential Calculus from a More General Point of View*.\n\nTao, T. (2016) *Analysis II*. 3rd edn. Singapore: Springer Singapore. doi:[10.1007/978-981-10-1804-6](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-10-1804-6) — ch. 6, *Several variable differential calculus*.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}